{"input": "In this text, the Doctor and Romana find themselves in a time-locked universe where they are reunited with Biroc. Biroc reveals that they were once slave masters who traveled through time to conquer planets and enslave their inhabitants. The Doctor and Romana are then returned to the null space where they are captured by Rorvik. Rorvik's crew realizes that the null space is shrinking and attempts to blast through the mirrors in the gateway to escape. However, their attacks are unsuccessful. With the gateway and the ship now within visible distance of each other, Rorvik decides to use the ship's engines to break the mirrors. The Doctor warns against this, but Romana, Lazlo, and Adric work together to free the remaining Tharils on the ship. As Rorvik initiates his plan, the blast from the engines is reflected back onto the ship, causing its destruction.", "answers": ["Paragraph 11"], "length": 7818, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "The abstract for this paragraph is: The task force attack on the base was a subterranean assault on the tunnel that connected the base held by the B team"}
{"input": "Morgan signed a five-year contract with the Washington Redskins, but the last three years of the contract were voidable. He became the starting wide receiver for the team in the 2012 season. In a game against the St. Louis Rams, Morgan was penalized for unsportsmanlike conduct after throwing the ball at an opposing player. He was fined by the NFL for this incident. However, he later scored a touchdown and his first receiving touchdown of the season in subsequent games.", "answers": ["Paragraph 18"], "length": 10417, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "Okubo is a Japanese football player who was born in Kanda, Fukuoka on June 9, 1982. After completing high school, he joined the J1 League team Cerezo Osaka in 2001. In his first season, Okubo played as a forward and offensive midfielder, but the team performed poorly and finished at the bottom of the league, resulting in relegation to the J2 League. However, in the following season (2002), Okubo scored 18 goals and helped Cerezo Osaka secure second place, allowing them to return to the J1 League after just one year in the lower division. From 2003 onwards, Okubo consistently scored more than 15 goals each season in the J1 League. Additionally, in 2003, he was honored with the Asian Young Footballer of the Year award.", "answers": ["Paragraph 9"], "length": 8644, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The Presbyterian Church of the United States started a medical missionary work in Firozpur in 1894. Rev. Janvier Newton and his wife, Frances, arrived in 1877 and started a small dispensary for men. Mrs. Frances Newton opened a dispensary for women and children from their residence, and later collected funds to build a hospital for women and children. The Frances Newton Hospital opened in 1894 with 50 beds, and a School of Nursing was opened in 1923. Dr. Maud Allen, Dr. Grace Edwards, and Dr. Dorothy Ferris served as Medical Superintendents of the hospital throughout the years. Dr. Ferris, who had received medical training in the United States, spent a year in Language schools before coming to India.", "answers": ["Paragraph 16"], "length": 8429, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "This text provides a summary of the path of the Leach river. It starts in a limestone valley and reaches the first settlement called Northleach. In Northleach, it flows out of a Victorian conduit and is also known as the Seven Springs. The first watermill on the river is located in a part of Northleach called Mill End. The river runs along a section of mill race stonework close to the churchyard, marking the town boundary. It can be seen again at a road bridge at the end of the town, still small in size. The river then continues down the valley, passing through the hamlet of Eastington alongside a lane. Eventually, it flows through a culvert and grazing land.", "answers": ["Paragraph 8"], "length": 8931, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1: The story begins with the birth of Kumar Venkateshwaran on June 25, 1985."}
{"input": "The text provides a summary of various characters in the Skaven, a fictional race in the Warhammer Fantasy Battle universe. These characters include Deathmaster Snikch, the chief assassin and agent of Lord Sneek; Grey Seer Thanquol, a powerful and scheming Grey Seer often thwarted by other characters; Ikit Claw, a loyal member of clan Skryre focused on the study of magery; Lurk Snitchtongue, a servant of Thanquol who attempts to betray his master; Plague Lord Nurglitch, the first plague lord of clan Pestilens; Lord Skrolk, a former Plague Monk who rises in power; Skweel Gnawtooth, a skilled packmaster of clan Moulder; Thrott the Unclean, a mutant and master mutator of Clan Moulder; Tretch Craventail, the Clanchief of clan Rictus known for his cunning; VerminLord Skreech Verminking, the avatar of the Horned Rat and the most powerful Skaven; and Warlord Queek Head-taker, the right claw of Warlord Gnawdwell and a powerful warlord in the Under-Empire.", "answers": ["Paragraph 27"], "length": 9098, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The surname \"Gyllenhaal\" originated from Gunne Olofsson Haal, who was from the Hahlegården farm estate in Sweden. The name was spelled in different ways, including \"Gyllenhahl\" and \"Gyllenhaal.\" The use of the prefix \"Gyllen\" meaning \"Golden\" was common in noble titles since the 16th century. The spelling inconsistencies were typical of the time, before Swedish spelling became more standardized.", "answers": ["Paragraph 7"], "length": 8751, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The text discusses the military service of an individual named Eddleman during World War II. Eddleman initially enrolled at the University of Illinois but was called to join the Army Air Corps in January 1943. He was stationed in Miami Beach and served as a physical trainer for new cadets. While in Florida, he suffered a serious foot injury during a beach volleyball game. After approximately eighteen months, Eddleman returned to his home state and was stationed at Scott Field near Belleville, Illinois. His final destination was Wright Field in Dayton, Ohio, where he played basketball for the Kittyhawks, a highly regarded military service \"all-star\" team. During his time with the Kittyhawks, Eddleman was named to the College All-Star team and played games across the country, including a memorable victory against the Harlem Globetrotters in 1945. In the fall of 1946, Eddleman completed his military service and returned to his college life as a student athlete.", "answers": ["Paragraph 26"], "length": 9296, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The text discusses the issue of Latvia's borders and its relationship with Russia. The Latvian constitution states that borders are determined by international treaties, with the 1920 Treaty of Rīga being considered still in force. In 1990, Latvia declared its independence and stated that it would base its relations with Russia on treaty principles. However, Russia does not acknowledge Latvia's previous occupation and incorporation into the USSR, viewing it as a newly independent country. Western countries have not recognized the legality of Latvia's incorporation into the USSR, but there is pressure for both countries to resolve the issue. The need for a border agreement is also highlighted as it could affect Russia's visa policy with the EU.", "answers": ["Paragraph 24"], "length": 9412, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "In 1939, a man named Valentino, while serving in the Navy, played several matches for the Navy team and impressed observers from Venezia. He was invited to tryout for the team and convinced the manager to purchase him. After a period in the reserve team, he was hired in January 1940. He made his debut in Serie A as a substitute and played in the remaining league games, helping Venezia avoid relegation. He also scored a goal in a Coppa Italia match.", "answers": ["Paragraph 20"], "length": 9968, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The text describes a wrestling event where two factions, Los Ingobernables and Los Guerreros Lagunero, face off for the third week in a row. Los Ingobernables come to the ring dressed in street gang attire rather than their usual wrestling gear. The match focuses on Último Guerrero and Rush, who have an upcoming singles match. Los Ingobernables lose due to a disqualification but continue to show their intention of inflicting pain on their opponents. In another match, Volador Jr. dresses as Spider-Man but loses to La Fuerza TRT. The third match involves a long-running rivalry between Máximo and Rey Escorpión, which started when Rey defeated Máximo's father in a bet match. Máximo interferes and costs his father the match. The feud includes a championship match won by Rey but is reignited when Máximo wins an intense match against Rey. The fourth match includes Mephisto, who dresses as Zombie Batman, teaming up with Ephesto and Hechicero against rookie Dragon Lee and Los Reyes de la Atlantida. The teams split the first two falls and a potential future storyline is hinted at between Hechicero and Guerrero Maya, Jr.", "answers": ["Paragraph 29"], "length": 9105, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "Broadway Boogie Woogie is a painting by Piet Mondrian completed in 1943. It is characterized by a grid of squares, inspired by the city grid of Manhattan and boogie-woogie music. The painting was bought for $800 by the Brazilian sculptor Maria Martins and later donated to the Museum of Modern Art in New York City.", "answers": ["Paragraph 28"], "length": 8924, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "In June 1917, Shepherd joined the 5th Marine Regiment as part of the American Expeditionary Forces and sailed to France. He arrived in Saint-Nazaire on June 27 and became part of the 4th Marine Brigade in the 2nd Division, which was organized on October 26. The 2nd Division was under the command of Marine Corps Brigadier General Charles A. Doyen, who was previously the commander of the 5th Marines. The division trained with French Army veterans during the winter of 1917-18.", "answers": ["Paragraph 6"], "length": 9991, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "This text discusses the unique status of Theseus in the cultic calendar of Athens and his representation of Athenian traits. Despite being born in Megara and being considered an outsider initially, Theseus became representative of essential Athenian qualities. It is suggested that his actions and journey to Crete came to symbolize the naval power of Athens after the Persian Wars. The text also highlights the dynamic nature of Athenian festivals, as new interpretations of founding myths can lead to new understandings of the festivals. The Pyanopsia festival, along with other Theseus-based Greek festivals, not only symbolically thanked Apollo for his actions and abundance in agriculture, but also represented the values of the founders. The text also mentions the worship of the Horae, the daughters of the goddess of divine law and order, at the Pyanopsia festival. This connection between the Horae and agriculture and vegetation is revealed through their worship at the Pyanopsia and another spring festival called the Thargelia.", "answers": ["Paragraph 1"], "length": 10601, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The text provides a history of radio station WULM and its various formats over the years. Originally known as WJEL, the station was founded as a country music station. After being sold to local businessmen, the call letters were changed to WBLY and the station operated as a talk and music station, with a popular host named Smilin' Bob. WBLY also aired a top-rated Sunday morning big band program. In 1981, the format changed to classic country and later switched to oldies. The station was eventually \"sold\" to Ron Yontz and moved to a new location, which improved its coverage but was detrimental to the AM station's signal. In 1988, WBLY received authorization to operate 24 hours a day. To celebrate, staff members hosted a 24-hour broadcast from the station's street studio.", "answers": ["Paragraph 4"], "length": 8559, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "In this text, several events and conflicts unfold in the world of Osman Bey, the founder of the Ottoman Empire. Aya Nikola becomes the new Tekfur of İnegöl, while Ertuğrul returns to the tribe. Yavlak Arslan seeks to create his own state and sees Osman as an obstacle. They later join forces against a new threat from the Han of the İlhanlı, who allies with Nikola against the Turks of Anatolia. Bala faces the arrival of Targun, Nikola's spy who later allies with Osman. Osman is elected as the new Bey after his father's death and decides to marry a second wife. A major battle with the Byzantines occurs, resulting in the death of Osman's nephew Bayhoca. Osman tries to find a traitor within the Kayı tribe with the help of Ömer Bey, and they face threats from Kara Şaman Togay. Malhun Hatun is captured and later released by Nikola. Osman captures Nikola's ally Tekfur Aris but Togay kills him, keeping Malhun captive. Togay then delivers an ultimatum to surrender the Tekfur in exchange for Malhun, but Osman plans to trap Nikola instead. However, Togay kills Aris, giving Nikola an excuse to keep Malhun captive. Osman initiates a major battle with the Byzantines, with the support of Malhun Hatun. Dündar is revealed as a traitor and is sentenced to death. Osman eventually wins the war with the Byzantines, and his reception by Sultan Mesud II angers Ömer Bey. Nikola betrays Ömer, and Togay ambushes him, but he is saved by Osman. Osman marries Malhun Hatun to improve relations. Togay attacks the Kayı tribe during their migration, resulting in the death of Bamsi Beyrek. Osman takes revenge by killing Togay. Nikola prepares for a great war and faces Osman in battle, but Osman emerges victorious, earning respect among the other beys in Bithynia.", "answers": ["Paragraph 29"], "length": 10041, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "According to Stephen Oppenheimer, the gene pool of the British Isles has not been significantly impacted by invasions since Roman times. He suggests that the inhabitants of the British Isles from prehistoric times belong to an Iberian genetic grouping, with similarities to the Basque people. Oppenheimer also proposes that the division between the West and East of England is not due to the Anglo-Saxon invasion, but rather to two main routes of genetic flow after the Last Glacial Maximum. He claims that Celtic languages split from Indo-European earlier than previously believed and that the English language separated from other Germanic languages before the Roman period. Bryan Sykes has also arrived at similar conclusions. The National Museum of Wales, however, notes that early genetic studies have produced unreliable conclusions based on limited samples and outdated assumptions.", "answers": ["Paragraph 11"], "length": 8851, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The text describes the history of the Pandya Kingdom of Tamilakam and its subsequent migration to Kerala. The Pandya Kingdom was attacked by Malik Kafur, the commander-in-chief of Alauddin Khalji, prompting two branches of the dynasty to flee to the west of Kerala to protect themselves. One branch settled in Poonjar, while the other branch faced many difficulties before settling in Pandalam. The Chembazhannur branch initially settled in Valliyur and later Tenkasi but faced threats of invasion, leading them to move to Puliyankudi. They eventually settled in Konni, constructing a shrine for Lord Shiva and establishing themselves as the ruling class. Due to attacks by the Cholas, they moved to Pandalam, where they established a full-fledged kingdom. The Pandalam rulers maintained friendly relations with the rajas of Travancore, particularly through their close friendship with Kunjunni Varma Thampan.", "answers": ["Paragraph 7"], "length": 10303, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "This text provides a brief summary of the life and career of Lloyd-Jones. He was born in Cardiff, Wales in 1899 and grew up in Llangeitho. His father was a grocer and he had two brothers. One brother died during the flu pandemic in 1918, while the other became a High Court judge. Lloyd-Jones attended a London grammar school and then studied medicine at St Bartholomew's Hospital. He worked as an assistant to the Royal Physician and obtained a medical degree from the University of London. After feeling a calling to preach, he returned to Wales in 1927 and married Bethan Phillips. He accepted an invitation to minister at a church in Aberavon, Port Talbot. They had two children together.", "answers": ["Paragraph 22"], "length": 9065, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "In the year 1800, a group of Agsalog (Igorot) people in Golot, Mountain Province decided to leave their impoverished and troubled homeland. They held a secret meeting and divided themselves into three groups, with each group heading in a different direction. Before beginning their journey, they performed religious rites and offered sacrifices to appease the gods. The group that traveled to the west eventually arrived at a place called Lidlidda. Along the way, they faced challenges such as difficult terrain and hunger. After twenty days, they found a suitable location to settle, with abundant resources such as fish, wild animals, and fruit. They believed that this place would allow them to establish a peaceful and prosperous community, prompting them to stop their nomadic lifestyle and settle there.", "answers": ["Paragraph 8"], "length": 8976, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "According to Robert Vane Russell, many gotras (clans) in the Hindu religion have names that originate from plants, animals, and natural objects. These totemic names are common among tribes but also exist in Hindu castes. The most common totem names are animals, including those that are considered sacred by Hindus. There is a wide variety of names, including trees, crops, salt, and household objects. The names of certain rishis (sages) are also associated with animals and plants. The characteristic of totemism is that members of a clan see themselves as related to the animals or trees from which the clan gets its name and refrain from killing or eating them.", "answers": ["Paragraph 28"], "length": 8863, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "Bell miners are a type of bird that mainly feed on insects called psyllids and their secretions. Psyllid products make up a large part of the bell miner's diet, sometimes comprising up to 90% of it. They primarily forage in the canopy, but also descend to the understory. There is a theory that bell miners \"farm\" psyllids by excluding other psyllid-eating bird species from their territory. This theory suggests that bell miners may selectively eat older nymphs or lerps, leaving the nymphs unharmed. The evidence for this theory has been mixed, with some studies supporting it and others not. However, when bell miners are removed from an area, other bird species quickly consume the psyllid colonies that were previously protected by the miners.", "answers": ["Paragraph 23"], "length": 9616, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "In his review of Greyhawk Adventures for Dragon magazine, Jim Bambra notes that the book is a collection of independent sections with little crossover. He mentions that the writing style reflects the numerous contributors, with some sections being lively and evocative while others are more dry and rules-focused. Bambra explains that while the book is aimed at World of Greyhawk fans, players of campaigns set in different worlds can still find interest in the new spells, magical items, and monsters. The descriptions of NPCs, deities, and clerics can also be used as templates for similar characters in other campaigns, and the adventures and section on zero-level characters are applicable to any campaign. Bambra suggests that some content may be less appealing to non-Greyhawk players, but it still provides inspiration. He praises the section on avatars but believes it could have been improved by including motivations for deities sending avatars. Bambra finds the information on playing clerics accessible but wishes there was more guidance on their conduct and associated myths. He compliments the section on monsters for its layout and presentation. Overall, Bambra states that Greyhawk Adventures tries to cater to a wide audience, but he feels it spreads itself too thin and adds little to the background of Oerth, the World of Greyhawk setting. However, he acknowledges that the book contains good material and is a useful purchase for those seeking new spells, items, and adventure inspiration.", "answers": ["Paragraph 8"], "length": 9063, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "In this text, it is mentioned that with war approaching, Monrad formed a government despite the advice of other National Liberal leaders. He became the Danish state leader during the early part of the Second Schleswig War but was criticized for being indecisive during critical moments. During an armistice, he let the king decide on a peace proposal that was ultimately rejected, leading to the continuation of the war and further military defeat. Monrad and his government were dismissed by the king, and the Peace of Vienna resulted in the loss of much of Denmark's territory and its relegation to a minor power. Monrad gave a speech in Parliament calling for continued resistance and against ratifying the peace treaty, even if it seemed like madness to do so.", "answers": ["Paragraph 11"], "length": 8464, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The text discusses the concept of credit rationing in a market with imperfect information. It cites the seminal work of Joseph Stiglitz and Andrew Weiss, who developed a model to show how credit rationing can be an equilibrium feature of the market. This means that a borrower may be willing to pay a higher interest rate than the lender charges, but the lender may not be willing to lend the extra funds due to lower expected profits.\n\nThe text also mentions that market equilibrium can be further enhanced by competition among lenders. In this case, lenders may be willing to accept higher risks than others, resulting in an equilibrium where borrowers who are willing to accept higher risks or pay higher rates can obtain loans. This can happen because lenders have different policies regarding adverse selection, where they face borrowers with different risk levels (types) without knowing the type of each borrower.\n\nThe main idea behind this equilibrium is that safe borrowers would not be willing to accept a high interest rate because they have a low probability of default and will likely end up paying back a large amount. On the other hand, risky borrowers may accept a higher rate because they have a lower chance of a successful project and lower chances of repayment. In this case, the cost of borrowing, or the interest rate paid, increases to account for the additional risk.\n\nThe text concludes by mentioning that when a lender restricts their lending and keeps rates lower, this assumes limited liability and supports the concept of credit rationing. However, the results mentioned in the text could still hold even with unlimited liability.", "answers": ["Paragraph 28"], "length": 9939, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The text describes the physical characteristics of a species of lizard. It focuses on details such as the large head with swollen cheeks in adult males, the length of the snout, the size and texture of the scales on different parts of the body, the presence or absence of certain features, and the shape and size of the limbs and tail.", "answers": ["Paragraph 16"], "length": 7539, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The text describes the activities of the Women Airforce Service Pilots (WASP) during World War II. The WASP were involved in various tasks such as towing targets for anti-aircraft artillery practice, simulating strafing missions, and transporting cargo. One particular assignment involved towing flying shooting targets for ground soldiers to practice shooting. During this training, many planes were shot, and a few WASP were accidentally shot in the feet. Additionally, the text mentions the tragic death of WASP Mabel Virginia Rawlinson, who was killed in a plane crash while practicing night flying with a faulty aircraft. The investigation into her death revealed that the planes used for towing were inadequately maintained, and the wrong octane fuel was being used.", "answers": ["Paragraph 2"], "length": 9321, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "This text provides a summary of the writing career and influence of Singaporean poet Edwin Thumboo. It notes that in the 1950s, Thumboo wrote lyrical poetry inspired by personal experiences and influenced by the English literary tradition. However, he shifted his focus to the public sphere in the 1970s, advocating for the creation of a national literature in post-independence Singapore. His collection of poetry, \"Gods Can Die,\" explored themes related to Singapore's national life. His subsequent anthologies, \"Ulysses by the Merlion\" and \"A Third Map,\" further established his reputation as a national poet committed to expressing a cultural vision for a multicultural Singapore. Thumboo is often referred to as Singapore's unofficial poet laureate due to his nationalistic-themed poems and his promotion of Singapore literature. His poem, \"Ulysses by the Merlion,\" was inspired by Irish mythology and history, particularly the works of W.B. Yeats. Thumboo considers himself a myth-inspired poet and views myths as narratives that provide stability in a multicultural society. The poem has also inspired other Singaporean poets to write their own Merlion-themed verses. A copy of \"Ulysses\" is installed on a plaque near the Merlion statue in Singapore.", "answers": ["Paragraph 1"], "length": 9976, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The German East Africa Company, also known as DOAG, was a colonial organization that played a significant role in the establishment of German East Africa, which encompassed modern-day Tanzania, Burundi, and Rwanda. Originally founded in 1884 as the Society for German Colonisation, the company aimed to engage in trade within Africa. It obtained a separate German sphere of influence in Wituland, within present-day Kenya, in 1885. The company leased a coastal strip opposite Zanzibar from Sultan Khalifa bin Said for 50 years, but its attempt to take over administration led to a revolt along the coast of Tanzania. With the assistance of the German navy, the company managed to maintain control of Dar es Salaam and Bagamoyo, but in 1889, it had to seek help from the German government to suppress the rebellion. Unable to effectively govern its dominions, the company sold out to the German government in 1891, which then assumed direct rule over German East Africa. The company continued to operate various activities, such as mines, plantations, railways, banking, and minting, until it relinquished them to the German colonial administration and other organizations. During World War I, British forces occupied German East Africa, bringing an end to the operations of the German East Africa Company.", "answers": ["Paragraph 2"], "length": 7814, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The text tells the story of three orphaned sisters who make a living by selling milk and butter from their female buffalo. When the buffalo disappears, the elder sister goes searching for it and encounters a little bird that asks her to marry it. She refuses and returns home. The middle sister also encounters the bird but declines its proposition. Finally, the youngest sister agrees to marry the bird and is led into a cave with magnificent rooms. The bird reveals that their buffalo was eaten by an evil raksha and the girl becomes the bird's wife. One day, she attends a festival and meets a handsome youth who is actually her husband in disguise. An old woman advises her to burn the bird's skin to reveal his true form. When she does, her husband, who is a prince, reappears and is taken away by a whirlwind. The girl sets out to find him and eventually does near a temple. He instructs her to make a new bird skin coat and chant a prayer to bring him back. She follows the instructions, and they live happily together.", "answers": ["Paragraph 28"], "length": 10274, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "Command guidance is a missile guidance system that relies on a ground or ship-based radar to track both the target and the missile. This system allows for the equipment necessary for missile guidance to be located at the launcher, where size and weight constraints are less important. The radar tracks the target and the missile separately, enabling them to follow independent and widely separated paths. A computer then calculates the position and velocity of both the target and the missile, determining an intercept point. Based on this information, the computer calculates the control inputs needed to guide the missile to the intercept point. Any necessary corrections are then sent to the missile via a radio signal, often using the radar tracking the missile as the radio signal.", "answers": ["Paragraph 7"], "length": 9091, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "This text is a summary of the career of a football player named Mason. He was drafted by the Oilers/Titans in 1997 and played in all 16 games in his rookie season. He had a total of 14 catches for 186 yards and made his NFL debut against the Oakland Raiders. In the following season, he played in all 16 games again and recorded 25 catches for 333 yards and three touchdowns. He also returned punts for 228 yards. In 1999, Mason had a successful season, gaining 1,030 yards returning punts and kickoffs and scoring a touchdown in the playoffs. He helped the Titans reach Super Bowl XXXIV, where he had a strong performance. In the 2000 season, he broke the NFL record for all-purpose yards in a single season and earned his first trip to the Pro Bowl. In 2001, he surpassed the 2,000 all-purpose yards mark for the second consecutive season and scored a career-high 9 touchdowns. He continued to excel in the following seasons, leading the team in various categories and earning high rankings in the NFL.", "answers": ["Paragraph 27"], "length": 10887, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The text explains that Jay Kay wrote the first track \"When You Gonna Learn\" while sending songs to record companies. The song was inspired by Native American and First Nation peoples, as well as Kay's anger towards the shooting of elephants. The track addresses various issues such as racism and corporate greed. After recording the song, Kay had a disagreement with his producer who made changes to the lyrics based on popular trends. This experience made Kay realize that he wanted a live band with a live sound. As a result, he named the band \"Jamiroquai\", combining the words \"jam\" and the Iroquois, a Native American confederacy.", "answers": ["Paragraph 16"], "length": 9628, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The doctrine of blood atonement, a controversial belief in Mormonism, suggests that Jesus' atonement does not redeem certain sins. According to this doctrine, individuals who commit eternal sins must be killed in a way that allows their blood to be shed upon the ground as a sacrificial offering. This is done to prevent them from becoming sons of perdition. However, the largest Mormon denomination, The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints, has rejected this doctrine since 1889. Early church leaders dismissed it as a \"fiction,\" while later leaders regarded it as a \"theoretical principle\" that was never practiced in the LDS Church.", "answers": ["Paragraph 1"], "length": 8060, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The electrification plebiscite in Alberta was the fourth province-wide plebiscite conducted. The ballot presented two options on electricity generation and transmission: creating a publicly-owned utility administered by the Alberta Government Power Commission, or leaving the industry in the hands of existing companies. The main issue was whether to provide rural electrification through public ownership or rely on private corporations that had not made much progress in that area. Despite the referendum result, the government still sponsored the creation of many Rural Electrification Associations, some of which continue to operate today.", "answers": ["Paragraph 26"], "length": 9395, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "Germany, despite having a socially conservative opposition party, is considered one of the most gay-friendly countries in the world. Recent polls show that a majority of Germans support same-sex marriage and accept homosexuality in society. Berlin is known as a particularly gay-friendly city, and several openly gay public figures have gained prominence in Germany. The Akademie Waldschlösschen, an education center, serves as a hub for various LGBTI groups and activists. Notably, Rosa von Praunheim's film \"It Is Not the Homosexual Who Is Perverse, But the Society in Which He Lives\" played a significant role in the gay liberation movement in Germany.", "answers": ["Paragraph 6"], "length": 8232, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The text provides a summary of the life and experiences of Goldring. He volunteered for the British Army in World War I but was discharged due to medical reasons. This led him to adopt a more critical stance towards the war from a socialist perspective. He became a member of the 1917 Club in Soho, a Bohemian radical club, and later moved to Dublin where he married his first wife and had two children. Goldring witnessed the funeral of an Irish Republican activist, Thomas Ashe, which he later wrote about in his book \"A Stranger in Ireland.\" Additionally, he was a member of the National Council for Civil Liberties.", "answers": ["Paragraph 15"], "length": 9132, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The condo unit owner and the building's developer may be held liable for negligence in the gas leak that led to a deadly explosion at Two Serendra condominium in Taguig. The leak was caused by the unauthorized movement of the gas range, which detached the gas supply hose. The explosion occurred when the leak was ignited by a light switch. The tenant was not alerted to the leak because the gas used in the pipeline system was odorless. The government investigation identified the property developer and the gas supplier as possibly liable, along with the owner and caretaker of the unit and the construction company that renovated the apartment. The Ayala-owned Serendra Inc. stated that it complied with regulations and promised to work with the government for improvements and adherence to any changes in regulations. The explosion resulted in four deaths and several injuries.", "answers": ["Paragraph 14"], "length": 8557, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The text describes the anatomical features of Euthecodon, a large crocodilian species with an elongated snout. It is noted that Euthecodon has unique characteristics, such as deeply scalloped rostral margins and teeth sitting in separate bony sheaths with a saw-like appearance. The species has slender, pointed teeth with two keels, indicating a piscivorous diet. The tooth count varies between species, leading some researchers to question whether they represent different species. Euthecodon typically has four premaxillary teeth, except for Euthecodon arambourgi, which has five teeth with the second one smaller. The forehead boss and raised nares are most prominent in E. brumpti and less developed in E. nitriae or E. arambourgi. The nasal bone is excluded from contact with the nares, and the skull table is small and square-shaped. Older individuals may develop squamosal ridges or \"horns\".", "answers": ["Paragraph 25"], "length": 9374, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The text discusses the involvement of Hay in the War of 1812, where he commanded the 100th Regiment of Foot at the Battle of Chippawa. During the battle, the British infantry suffered heavy casualties due to the American artillery firing canister shots. General Winfield Scott of the United States Army strategically positioned his brigade in a \"U\" shape, allowing his flanking units to attack the British from both sides. Hay fought bravely but was eventually taken prisoner by the Americans. After the war, Hay returned to Scotland and made improvements to his family estate. He served in various positions, including Pro-Grand Master of the Grand Lodge of Scotland and Lord Lieutenant of East Lothian. He also developed a patented method for making drainage tiles on his estate.", "answers": ["Paragraph 6"], "length": 9024, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The text discusses the analysis of the structure and timbre features of the composition \"Ionisation\" by Chou Wen-chung and Jean-Charles François. It mentions that only the last 17 measures of the composition include musical tones from the traditional tonal system. The text also mentions that Varèse's reinvention of pitch relationships between percussion instruments in \"Ionisation\" was a groundbreaking and previously unrecognized musical approach.", "answers": ["Paragraph 2"], "length": 8492, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "This text summarizes a series of events involving Mick, a character who is up for parole from a psychiatric hospital. Mick tries to reconcile with Irene, but she refuses to listen to him. Mick then apologizes to Billie's brother, Ash, who attacks him in return. Mick begs Irene for support but she declines. However, Mick is eventually granted parole and Irene helps him find a place to stay. Mick encounters Maggie, who invites him to her house, but Olivia reveals that Mick is a rapist, causing Maggie's husband to throw him out. Irene decides to cut ties with Mick, and he is offered a job in Western Australia but refuses to leave. Mick takes care of Luc, Irene's child, but collapses and takes Luc back to Irene's home. VJ takes Luc away from Mick, and Ash kidnaps Mick and threatens to kill him. Mick is saved by Hunter, Mason, and Brody. Mick approaches Ziggy for help, but she runs away and later recognizes Mick to the police. Mick suspects Ash plans to leave the Bay with Luc, but Irene reassures him that is not true. VJ does leave with Luc, prompting Mick to consider taking the job in WA. Irene joins him, and months later, Mick has an accident in the mines, prompting Irene to support him.", "answers": ["Paragraph 5"], "length": 8944, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "Lief, Barda, and Jasmine are on a mission to retrieve the gems of the Belt of Deltora to defeat the Shadow Lord. Along their journey, they encounter Ols, creatures created by the Shadow Lord, and are saved by a boy named Dain. They join the Resistance and undergo a test to prove they are not Ols. Dain helps them escape but is taken prisoner by pirates. They reach the Maze of the Beast, where Barda is revealed to be an Ol. They are captured by pirates and dumped into the Maze. They manage to retrieve the gem but are chased by the Beast. They escape through a blowhole, causing two pirates to die. They continue their journey to the Valley of the Lost.", "answers": ["Paragraph 12"], "length": 8243, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The text highlights the historical and ongoing opposition of the fossil fuel industry towards the nuclear industry. Starting in the 1950s, fossil fuel companies, such as the American Petroleum Institute and Marcellus Shale Coalition, engaged in lobbying efforts against nuclear power due to perceived threats to their commercial interests. In recent years, large fossil fuel suppliers have launched advertising campaigns presenting fossil gas as a complementary energy source to renewables.\n\nThe fossil fuel industry has also supported environmental organizations with anti-nuclear positions, including donating to Friends of the Earth. Some environmental groups, such as the Sierra Club and Environmental Defense Fund, have received grants from fossil fuel companies. Greenpeace previously advocated for the gradual replacement of nuclear power with fossil gas plants as a backup to renewable energy, but it has since shifted its stance towards grid energy storage.\n\nIn Germany, the Energiewende initiative, which aimed to transition to renewable energy, resulted in a gradual phaseout of nuclear power from 2000 to 2022. However, this led to an increase in fossil gas power production, with imports from Russia playing a significant role. Controversial pipeline projects, like Nord Stream 1, were constructed to meet the growing demand for Russian gas in Germany. Following the 2022 Russian invasion of Ukraine, it was revealed that Russian lobbying had influenced the anti-nuclear and anti-fracking movements in Germany.", "answers": ["Paragraph 12"], "length": 8452, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "After the September 11 attacks in 2001, Coleen Rowley, an FBI agent, wrote a paper for FBI Director Robert Mueller detailing how FBI personnel in Washington, D.C. mishandled information provided by the Minneapolis Field Office regarding their investigation into suspected terrorist Zacarias Moussaoui. Rowley's investigation revealed failures that may have made the U.S. vulnerable to the 9/11 attacks. She expressed frustration over the events that occurred leading up to the attacks and questioned why FBI agents would deliberately sabotage a case.", "answers": ["Paragraph 8"], "length": 8418, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "In 1879, a supposed petrified body of a seven-foot-tall man was uncovered during road work near the Taughannock House Hotel. The finding attracted attention, with Cornell University scientists examining parts of the body, and over 5,000 people paying to see the \"giant.\" However, it was later revealed to be a hoax orchestrated by the hotel's owner, John Thompson, and two others. The inspiration for the hoax came from the Cardiff Giant, a similar fake discovery in Cardiff, New York in 1869. Although the original giant was damaged and lost, a replica was made in 2019 for the Tompkins Center for History & Culture.", "answers": ["Paragraph 20"], "length": 9471, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The text provides a summary of the opening and operation of the West Side and Yonkers Patent Elevated Railway Company. The railway line started operating on July 3, 1868, and was considered a successful experiment. It was initially called the West Side and Yonkers Patent Elevated Railway Company and later renamed. The line had a northern terminus at 29th Street and a southern terminus at Dey Street. Steam engines located in nearby buildings drove multiple long cable loops that were activated when a car approached and stopped once it passed. The cars connected to the cables using claws, which resulted in jerks each time they moved to the next cable.", "answers": ["Paragraph 25"], "length": 8888, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The text provides a summary of some notable film roles taken on by an actor named Yam in the late 1980s and 1990s. In 1989, Yam starred in the movie Bloodfight, which was a unique Japanese-Hong Kong co-production because it was entirely spoken in English. He gained critical acclaim in 1992 for his role as Judge in the crime film Full Contact, where he engaged in a violent battle against Chow Yun-fat's character. Yam also appeared as \"Dhalsim\" in the action-comedy Future Cops in 1993, a parody of the Street Fighter franchise. Additionally, he played Chiang Tin-Sung, the leader of the Hung Hing triads, in the first three films of the Young and Dangerous series, starting in 1996.", "answers": ["Paragraph 23"], "length": 8088, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "During the reign of Charles VI, Eleonore and her daughter-in-law Wilhelmine Amalia were involved in a succession dispute on behalf of Joseph I's daughters. A secret pact called the Mutual Pact of Succession was signed in 1703, stating that if both brothers died without male heirs, the daughters of the elder brother would take precedence over the daughters of the younger brother. The surviving sisters would also be heiresses. This pact was known only to a few individuals, including Leopold I and his sons. Eleonore and Wilhelmine were aware of its existence and worked to establish a public succession order. In 1712, Wilhelmine persuaded Count Seilern to give her the document, which she sent to her family's head. They then sought assistance from Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz to negotiate with Charles VI. Eventually, Charles VI recognized the secret succession order in April 1713. However, just days prior, Charles had already expressed his desire to amend the pact to give his own future daughters precedence over his nieces.", "answers": ["Paragraph 30"], "length": 10318, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The text describes the events surrounding the expedition to conquer the Itza kingdom. Ursúa, the leader of the expedition, requested the support of three missionaries, who were joined by another group of Franciscans. The plan was to build a road connecting Yucatán with Guatemala, but due to the occupation of Sakbʼajlan by Guatemalan troops, Ursúa realized he was in a race to conquer the Itza kingdom. Ursúa issued new orders to Garcia, disguising his true intention to conquer the Itza, and directed him to the lake instead. Garcia then ordered the construction of a fort as the main military base for the project. Ursúa also authorized the creation of three new militia companies to reinforce Garcia.", "answers": ["Paragraph 7"], "length": 10754, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The Royal Alexandra Hospital for Sick Children in Brighton was opened in 1881 and used until 2007. It was known for its Queen Anne-style architecture and was a significant part of the community. Plans to demolish the building and replace it with luxury flats were met with opposition from residents and a local conservation group. The planning inspector ruled in favor of preserving the main building, and a compromise plan was approved in 2011. The main hospital building is currently being restored and converted into flats.", "answers": ["Paragraph 10"], "length": 8055, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "Uracil DNA glycosylases are enzymes that remove uracil from DNA. This uracil can occur through spontaneous deamination of cytosine or by the incorrect incorporation of dU opposite dA during DNA replication. The most well-known uracil DNA glycosylase is E. coli UDG. Mammalian cells have four different uracil-DNA glycosylase activities: UNG, SMUG1, TDG, and MBD4. These glycosylases differ in their substrate specificity and where they are located within the cell. \n\nSMUG1 has a preference for single-stranded DNA but can also remove uracil from double-stranded DNA. It can remove various forms of uracil, including 5-hydroxyuracil, 5-hydroxymethyluracil, and 5-formyluracil with oxidized groups at carbon 5. \n\nTDG and MBD4 are specific to double-stranded DNA. TDG can remove thymine glycol when it is opposite guanine and can also remove modified forms of uracil at carbon 5. MBD4 is specifically involved in repairing T:G mismatches that result from the deamination of 5-methylcytosine to thymine at CpG sites.\n\nIn human cells, TDG and SMUG1 are primarily responsible for repairing U:G mismatches caused by cytosine deamination, while UNG handles the repair of uracil arising from dU misincorporation. MBD4 is mostly involved in correcting T:G mismatches at CpG sites. Mutant MBD4 mice do not show increased susceptibility to cancer or reduced survival, but they do have more C to T mutations at CpG sequences in epithelial cells of the small intestine.", "answers": ["Paragraph 30"], "length": 9471, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The text describes the concept of \"zappers\" in the context of convergence culture, as discussed in Henry Jenkins' book. Zappers are individuals who frequently switch channels, watching snippets of shows rather than committing to a prolonged engagement. It is noted that being a zapper is more of a tendency rather than a defining characteristic, and it can vary depending on factors such as the content being aired and personal feelings towards a show. The text also mentions that the concept of zappers was popularized in the 1990s, with viewers being described as needing constant gratification and frequently switching channels.", "answers": ["Paragraph 9"], "length": 9415, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The text describes the character Silver from the television show \"90210\". Silver becomes friends with Annie Wilson and develops a romantic interest in Annie's adopted brother Dixon. She warns Annie not to trust Naomi Clark, revealing that they were once best friends. Silver also shares her troubled relationship with her mother. After a series of ups and downs in her relationship with Dixon, Silver's behavior becomes more erratic and she is diagnosed with bipolar disorder. She decides to transfer to a new school but faces judgment and bullying there. Eventually, she is crowned prom queen but realizes she is not suited for the role, causing a rift between her and Dixon. At a party, she interacts with Ethan, who admits his feelings for her.", "answers": ["Paragraph 17"], "length": 10382, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "Jay Nichols is Herman's best friend and a writer for Waterton. He helped Herman get a job as a fact checker in the research department. Jay is confident and a ladies' man, often pursuing women with his clichéd pick-up lines. Despite periodic rejection, Jay admires Herman's cautious and nice guy persona. However, Jay's higher position in the company sometimes makes Herman feel insecure. Initially, Jay is seen chasing after flashy women, but he later falls in love with his ex-fiancée who had left him at the altar. She abandons him again, but Jay pretends he left her. In the third season, Herman sets Jay up with Louise Fitzer, and they fall in love.", "answers": ["Paragraph 30"], "length": 10098, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The text provides a summary of Thomas' coaching career in basketball. It mentions his roles as an undergraduate at the University of Dayton, where he worked with the basketball team, assisted with player workouts, and was involved in video exchange and scouting reports. He then joined the University of Pittsburgh at Johnstown as head assistant coach, recruiting and academic coordinator, and opponent scout. He later became an administrative assistant for the RedHawks of Miami University, helping with film exchange, study tables, and travel arrangements. He had successful seasons with the team, winning the Mid-American Conference tournament and appearing in the NCAA tournament. Thomas also worked as an NBA Advance Scout for teams such as the Golden State Warriors, Chicago Bulls, and Utah Jazz. He served as an assistant coach for the Slovenia national basketball team and had coaching positions with various teams in Canada, China, Malaysia, Slovenia, and Japan. Currently, he is the Head Coach of the Guaynabo Mets in Puerto Rico and led the team to a Semifinals appearance in the playoffs.", "answers": ["Paragraph 10"], "length": 10972, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The Thistles, a hockey team in the Manitoba intermediate league, had a successful season in 1899-1900 and finished second. They continued their success in the following season, winning the league title and dominating their opponents. However, the club executives were worried that if the games were not competitive, fans would not attend and the team would generate less revenue. In an effort to prove their strength, they applied to join the senior Manitoba Hockey Association, which had two teams in Winnipeg. The Thistles played an exhibition match against the Victorias, a top team in Canada and previous Stanley Cup winner, and performed well. However, the two Winnipeg teams rejected their application, stating that it was too late in the season. The Thistles returned to the intermediate league, but faced injuries to key players and finished tied for second place. One of their best players, Tommy Phillips, left the team to attend McGill University in Montreal.", "answers": ["Paragraph 26"], "length": 8937, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "This text discusses the debate among watch experts about the accuracy and value of tourbillons in modern mechanical watches. While some argue that tourbillons never improved accuracy, they are still highly valued by collectors and are often found in premium-priced watches. Swiss luxury watchmakers produce high-quality tourbillon wristwatches that can cost tens or even hundreds of thousands of dollars. However, in recent years, there has been an increase in availability and a slight decrease in prices for basic tourbillon models. Nonetheless, watches with tourbillons are still significantly more expensive than those without. Swiss models typically start at $40,000, while some Chinese models can range from hundreds to nearly $5000.", "answers": ["Paragraph 27"], "length": 8275, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "The abstract is:"}
{"input": "William Hart was born in Scotland and emigrated to the United States in 1831. He started his artistic career as a decorative painter, working on coach panels. He also dabbled in portrait painting but was unsuccessful in finding commissions. He exhibited his first work at the National Academy of Design in 1848 and then traveled to Europe to study landscape painting. He primarily painted in Scotland until 1852 before returning to Albany and then moving to New York City in 1853. In New York, he focused on landscape painting and opened a studio in the Tenth Street Studio Building. In 1865, he became President of the Brooklyn Academy of Design.", "answers": ["Paragraph 26"], "length": 9032, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "This text discusses the industrialization and railroad development in the city of Spokane. The Olmsted Brothers, who were developing a master plan for parks in the city, noted the presence of railroads in the downtown area in 1908. They sarcastically mentioned that the area which later became the site of Expo '74 had already been partially \"improved\" with industrial development. They hoped that Spokane would eventually reclaim the area around Spokane Falls for a park. Over time, Spokane became the site of four transcontinental railroads and regional ones. Union Pacific built their own station on the park's site by 1914. However, having a large number of railroads in the city caused problems, such as restricted access to the Spokane River and noise pollution. The presence of elevated tracks, warehouses, and at-grade railroad crossings led to physical and visual limitations.", "answers": ["Paragraph 8"], "length": 7990, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "In 1960, Karpiński's AKAT-1 invention earned him recognition as Poland's candidate for the UNESCO worldwide award for young engineers. Out of approximately 200 contestants, Karpiński was chosen as one of six laureates. As a result, he was granted a half-year scholarship in the United States, which was later extended to a full year. Karpiński visited various universities and laboratories, including prestigious institutions such as Harvard, Caltech, UCLA, and Los Alamos National Laboratory. He had the opportunity to meet prominent computer scientists such as John Eckert, Claude Shannon, and Edward F. Moore. Despite receiving tempting offers to remain in the United States, Karpiński decided to return to Poland. Controversy arose when it was revealed that Karpiński had collaborated with Polish intelligence before and during his time in the US. However, Karpiński, in post-communist interviews, hesitated to discuss the matter but asserted that his agreement with intelligence officer cpt. Zygmunt Goć was confined to reporting on the advancements in American technical facilities.", "answers": ["Paragraph 30"], "length": 9969, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The text discusses the role of race in health disparities in the United States. It states that there have historically been significant disparities in health and access to healthcare between races, and current evidence supports the continuation of these disparities. These disparities include differences in the quality of care and overall insurance coverage based on race. The text cites a study that found that blacks receive lower quality care than white individuals. The lower quality of care for minority groups is attributed to factors such as low-income and poverty. The text also highlights the higher death rates for African Americans, particularly in relation to treatable or preventable causes such as heart disease and breast cancer. Additionally, infant mortality rates for African American and Latino infants are 2 to 3 times higher than other racial groups. Overall, race is identified as a significant factor contributing to health disparities in the United States.", "answers": ["Paragraph 13"], "length": 9356, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "In Act II of the play, Brassbound, an Italian member of Brassbound's crew, is injured in a feud, and Lady Cicely takes care of him. Sir Howard complains about Brassbound's behavior, but Brassbound reveals that he is actually Sir Howard's nephew and accuses him of causing the death of his mother and taking away his inheritance. Brassbound plans to hand Sir Howard over to a fanatical Islamist Sheik as revenge. Lady Cicely argues with Brassbound, pointing out the brutality of his own sense of justice. Eventually, Brassbound agrees to give up revenge. However, when the Sheik arrives, he offers to buy Lady Cicely instead of accepting any other price. Lady Cicely agrees, but then the local ruler appears and arrests Brassbound, setting Sir Howard free.", "answers": ["Paragraph 12"], "length": 9827, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "This text describes the managerial career of Charlie Grimm with the Chicago Cubs. After Rogers Hornsby was fired in 1932, Grimm took over as player-manager and led the team to a successful season, winning the National League pennant. Despite being swept by the New York Yankees in the World Series, Grimm retained his position as manager for the next seven seasons. During his tenure, the Cubs consistently finished in the top three of their league standings. In 1935, Grimm led the team to their first 100-win season in 25 years, including a 21-game winning streak in September. Despite this success, they lost to the Detroit Tigers in the World Series. Grimm's first tenure as manager ended in 1938 when he was moved to the broadcast booth, and Gabby Hartnett took over as player-manager.", "answers": ["Paragraph 24"], "length": 8886, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "Beulah Park, a horse racing track, was owned by the Robert Dienst family for 60 years from 1923 to May 1983. Robert J. Dienst, the original owner, was known for his famous horse Decathlon and for making gambling fun for the public. After his death, his son Robert Y. Dienst took over and rebuilt the track, especially after a fire in 1959. He focused on improving the facilities for both the public and horsemen, including building new barns with better materials. Robert Y. was a passionate horseman and continued racing with his own stable until his death in 1996. His son Bob M. Dienst left Beulah Park in 1981 due to a disagreement with the general manager and now works in real estate. Robert Y. Dienst's son-in-law Tom Heinl worked on Parimutuels from 1975 to 1986, computerizing the Totalization equipment and revolutionizing the way parimutuel tickets are sold.", "answers": ["Paragraph 6"], "length": 7953, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The text discusses the various works and accomplishments of a writer named Scott. In addition to writing plays, including The Vicarage, The Cape Mail, Anne Mié, Odette, and The Great Divorce Case, Scott also adapted plays by Victorien Sardou and Halévy and Meilhac. His collaborations with B. C. Stephenson were particularly successful, with their adaptation of Dora receiving acclaim and playing in both London and New York. Scott and Stephenson also adapted a libretto for Lecocq's operetta Le Petit Duc, impressing the composer enough that he agreed to write new music for the English production. Scott used the pseudonym \"Saville Rowe\" for these works. In addition to his theatrical writing, Scott wrote about his travel experiences, which influenced his creative writing. It is also mentioned that he may have written the tune to the \"Swiss Cradle Song,\" later adapted as \"Now Is the Hour\" and as \"Haere Ra,\" a Maori farewell song. However, there is some debate over the true identity of the composer.", "answers": ["Paragraph 24"], "length": 9019, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "In 2005, a cooperative contract was signed with Mobil Cepu Ltd., a subsidiary of ExxonMobil, to officially begin the exploration of the Cepu Block oil fields in Bojonegoro, Indonesia. Pertamina, the state oil company, also signed a joint operation agreement with ExxonMobil in 2006. Under a 30-year production-sharing contract, Pertamina and ExxonMobil would each have a 45 percent interest in the block, with the remaining 10 percent going to local governments. Foreign companies, particularly from China, have invested around $8 million in various projects related to the exploration and exploitation of the oil fields. Dorodjatun Kuntjoro-Jakti, the former minister and ambassador to the United States, believes that Bojonegoro and Tuban could become similar to Texas due to their abundant gas and oil resources.", "answers": ["Paragraph 25"], "length": 9668, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "In the month of December 2017, Mumbai City FC had several matches in the Indian Super League. They began with a draw against Kerala Blasters, with both teams scoring one goal each. Mumbai then faced Chennaiyin and secured a 1-0 victory, ending Chennaiyin's winning streak. The team went on to defeat ATK 1-0, with ATK's goal coming from a shot deflected by Mumbai's Robin. In their match against NorthEast United, Mumbai won 2-0 with Balwant Singh scoring both goals. The month ended with Mumbai routing Delhi Dynamos 4-0, with goals from Lucian Goian, Everton Santos, and Balwant Singh. The match also saw red cards for players from both teams.", "answers": ["Paragraph 24"], "length": 9446, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The text describes a football game between the Stallions and the Stars. In the fourth quarter, the Stars attempt a field goal but miss, giving the ball to the Stars. The Stars score a touchdown and a two-point conversion, taking the lead for the first time in the game. The Stallions then throw an interception, giving the Stars another opportunity to increase their lead. However, they are unable to score. The Stallions then score a touchdown but miss the extra point, bringing the game within three points. The Stars attempt a risky play on fourth down but fail, giving possession back to the Stallions. The Stallions are unable to gain many yards and punt the ball. On the Stars' next possession, they throw an interception, securing the victory for the Stallions. The game ends with the Stallions winning 33–30.", "answers": ["Paragraph 1"], "length": 9333, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The text describes the events that transpire after Godefroy de Montmirail is interrupted at his wedding by news of his bride's gravely ill father. Consulting a wizard, he learns that the corridors of time are being held open, causing harm to his future father-in-law. Determined to find the culprit, Godefroy embarks on a mission. In the present day, two characters named Jacquouille and Ginete rob a supermarket and eventually end up working for Beatrice. Beatrice's husband, Jean-Pierre, a dentist, scares away his patients, leading Beatrice to allow Jacquouille to watch TV, which he believes is the work of Satan and destroys. Jacquart, another character, is tortured by the Inquisition. Godefroy intervenes to stop the torture and admits to Burning Jacquart's Range Rover. Jacquouille and Ginette receive a potion from Beatrice that sends them back to the year 1123 where they help Godefroy fight invaders. They discover that Jacquouille has stolen the Duke's treasure, and the remaining items are in the future, keeping the corridors of time open. They return to their own time, terrorize a hotel, and retrieve the stolen jewels from Jacqouille's ex-wife. They later attend a wedding and cause chaos before mistakenly being sent to 18th-century France during the French Revolution. Jacquouille recognizes his descendant, Jacqouillet, as Napoleon Bonaparte's advisor, but they are arrested as aristocrats.", "answers": ["Paragraph 25"], "length": 9006, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The author reflects on a defining moment in their life when their father served in Vietnam. Their family went through difficult times during this period, and eventually, they left their father and moved to live with their grandmother. The author admits to having resentments towards their father for not being present, but they also started to think about his perspective and what he might have gone through. The author then used music as an outlet to express these thoughts and emotions, ultimately creating a song that deeply resonated with their father. The song brought the author and their father closer together and had a positive impact on their relationship.", "answers": ["Paragraph 2"], "length": 10134, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
{"input": "The forming process is commonly used to change the size of cylindrical tubing, but it can also be used to shape sheet metal by pushing it onto a die at a high speed. It can create strong connections either by crimping with a mechanical interlock or by welding. The mass of the work piece is important in this process because of the high acceleration and deceleration involved. While it is most effective with materials like copper or aluminum, it can also be used with steel.", "answers": ["Paragraph 27"], "length": 8214, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "The abstract states that the forming process is used to change the shape of cylindrical tubing, such as pipes, by repelling it against a shaped die. It can"}
{"input": "In February 2004, Chanté released her third single \"Bad Boy\", which was successful and reached the Top 10 on radio. The accompanying music video, directed by Director X, reached No. 1 on the MuchMusic chart. In June 2004, Chanté released her fourth single \"Does He Love Me?\" featuring Foxy Brown, which also achieved success on radio and reached the top 5. The music video for this song also reached No. 1 on MuchMusic and won Chanté an Urban Music Video Award. In June 2004, Chanté released her self-titled debut album, which was later certified gold in December of that year. At the Canadian Urban Music Awards in October 2004, Chanté won several awards, including \"Best New Artist\" and \"Video of the Year\". Her success caught the attention of BET President Stephen Hill, who became her mentor and promoted her music video for \"Bad Boy\". In the same month, Chanté performed the national anthem at the Canadian Football League's Grey Cup championship game and was chosen to represent Canada at Expo 2005 in Japan.", "answers": ["Paragraph 4"], "length": 10149, "dataset": "passage_retrieval_en", "pred": "Paragraph 1"}
